From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ios-blink-sideload <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 23:28:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] iOS sideload: request desktop site

Chrome's "Request Desktop Site" did nothing: it calls
NavigationManager::ReloadWithUserAgentType(), which was
NOTIMPLEMENTED() for Blink, and items always said they were mobile.

Do it as Chrome on Android does
(NavigationControllerAndroid::SetUseDesktopUserAgent()): the first time,
give the WebContents a desktop user agent override (Chrome on the
Mac's, with client hints to match, where Android uses Linux's); mark
the last committed entry as overriding the user agent and load its
original request URL again. Links and addresses opened from it keep the
override (content inherits it), until Request Mobile Site. An item's
user agent type is now its entry's override flag, so the menu offers
the other mode.

Status: verified at Chromium 4558926c in the iOS 27 simulator, with a
test page that reports the User-Agent header, navigator.userAgent and
navigator.userAgentData: Request Desktop Site reloads the page with
Chrome on the Mac's user agent in both, mobile false and platform macOS
in the client hints; reloading and following a link in the tab keep
it; the menu then offers Request Mobile Site, which reloads with the
iPhone user agent again. navigator.platform stays "iPhone", as it stays
"Linux armv81" on Android (the override does not change it).

diff --git a/ios/web/content/BUILD.gn b/ios/web/content/BUILD.gn
index 0af63b2bad3c7fc13cd7d1e74f653a88d5b41187..56750acb7f278013c2e560186ae344cccaddd8c2 100644
--- a/ios/web/content/BUILD.gn
+++ b/ios/web/content/BUILD.gn
@@ -53,6 +53,7 @@ source_set("content") {
